You extract only surface-level facts from a job posting. Do not evaluate fit, do not score anything, do not write reasoning. Be fast and literal - only use what is stated in the posting, except for the salary estimate described below.

JOB POSTING
{{ job_posting_text }}

LOCATION FORMAT
Always answer in English, using each place's one standard full name - never an abbreviation, local-language spelling, or alternate name. Examples of the ONLY acceptable forms: country "United States" (never "US", "USA", "America"), "United Kingdom" (never "UK", "England", "Britain"), "Russia" (never "Russian Federation", "РФ", "Россия"), "United Arab Emirates" (never "UAE"); state/region "California" (never "CA"); city "New York City" (never "NYC", "NY"). If the posting names a place in a non-English language (e.g. "Москва", "München"), translate it to its standard English form ("Moscow", "Munich"). This exact-name consistency is what lets postings from different companies be grouped by location later, and a wrong or inconsistent form silently breaks that grouping.

OUTPUT FORMAT
Respond using only the following tags, with no extra text outside them. Do not use markdown formatting inside the tags.

<company>Company name, or "N/A" if not stated</company>
<role>Job title as stated in the posting</role>
<location_country>The country, in its standard English name per LOCATION FORMAT above. Write "N/A" only if genuinely unknowable.</location_country>
<location_state>The state/province/region, if named. Omit this tag entirely if none is given - never guess one.</location_state>
<location_city>The city, if named. Omit this tag entirely if none is given - never guess one.</location_city>
<work_mode>Exactly one of: remote, hybrid, onsite, unclear. Read the full posting, not just the header - a header can say "Hybrid" while the body clarifies "we are also open to fully remote candidates"; when a later, more specific statement differs from a header line, the later statement wins.</work_mode>
<employment_type>Exactly one of: full_time, part_time, contract, internship, unclear</employment_type>
<salary_min>The minimum of the salary range, as a bare integer with ONLY digits - no currency symbols, no separators, no words like "k", "approx", "per year". Example: "100000", never "100k" or "$100,000". If the posting gives a single figure rather than a range, repeat that exact figure here as the minimum too. If salary is not stated in the posting at all, give your best estimated minimum for this role/seniority/company size/market, still as a bare integer</salary_min>
<salary_max>The maximum of the salary range, same bare-integer rules as salary_min. If the posting gives a single figure, repeat that same figure here as the maximum</salary_max>
<salary_currency>The ISO 4217 currency code and nothing else - exactly one of: USD, EUR, GBP, CHF, PLN, CZK, UAH, TRY, ILS, AED, INR, CNY, JPY, CAD, AUD, SGD, SEK, NOK, DKK, MXN, BRL, RSD, GEL, KZT. Pick the code matching the currency actually used or implied by the posting (stated symbol, or the country/company location if no symbol is given). If genuinely unclear, write "USD"</salary_currency>
<salary_period>Exactly one of: hour, month, year - matching how the figure is normally paid out for this kind of number (e.g. "25" per "hour", "8000" per "month", "120000" per "year"). Infer this from context even if not explicitly stated. Never write anything other than one of these three words</salary_period>
<salary_is_estimate>Exactly one of: true, false - "true" if the posting did not state a salary and you estimated it yourself, "false" if the posting explicitly stated it</salary_is_estimate>
<salary_original_text>The salary exactly as written in the posting, verbatim. If not stated, write "Not stated in posting - estimated based on role, seniority, company size and market"</salary_original_text>
<tag>A short keyword tag that adds information beyond the job title itself - a specific technology, tool, framework, niche domain, or a notable non-obvious condition (equity, on-call rotation, visa sponsorship, 4-day week). Never tag the role's own profession or broad domain when it is already obvious from the title (don't tag "AI" on an "AI Engineer" posting, or "Sales" on a "Sales Manager" posting) - anyone opening this posting already knows that. Repeat once per tag, up to 6 total. Omit entirely if nothing beyond the title is worth tagging.</tag>
